WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:06.294
Alright, quick poll --

00:00:06.628 --> 00:00:09.923
raise your hand if you have
a relationship in your life

00:00:09.923 --> 00:00:11.591
that's meaningful to you.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.805
OK, I assumed, but always good
to check our assumptions.

00:00:16.805 --> 00:00:17.973
(Laughter)

00:00:18.431 --> 00:00:19.683
I’m saying this because

00:00:19.724 --> 00:00:23.853
while I'll be focusing today
on a parent-child relationship,

00:00:23.895 --> 00:00:26.022
please know that everything
I'm talking about

00:00:26.022 --> 00:00:29.818
is applicable to any
meaningful relationship.

00:00:30.694 --> 00:00:33.488
So with that in mind, let's jump in.

00:00:34.781 --> 00:00:38.076
So it's Sunday night, I'm in my kitchen.

00:00:38.118 --> 00:00:40.537
I just finished cooking
dinner for my family,

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:42.706
and I am on edge.

00:00:43.039 --> 00:00:45.917
I mean, I'm exhausted,
I haven't been sleeping well.

00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:48.336
I’m anxious about the upcoming workweek,

00:00:48.378 --> 00:00:52.215
I'm overwhelmed by all the items
on my unfinished to-do list.

00:00:52.549 --> 00:00:54.759
And then, my son walks into the kitchen.

00:00:54.759 --> 00:00:57.178
He looks at the table and whines,

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.513
"Chicken again?"

00:00:58.513 --> 00:00:59.556
(Laughter)

00:00:59.598 --> 00:01:00.849
"Disgusting."

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:02.892
(Laughter)

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:04.477
And that's it. I snap.

00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:05.729
I look at him and I yell,

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.564
"What is wrong with you?

00:01:07.564 --> 00:01:10.567
Can you be grateful
for one thing in your life?"

00:01:12.068 --> 00:01:13.653
And things get worse from there.

00:01:13.653 --> 00:01:14.946
He screams, "I hate you."

00:01:14.988 --> 00:01:17.532
He runs out of the room
and he slams his bedroom door.

00:01:17.574 --> 00:01:20.035
And now, my self-loathing session begins,

00:01:20.035 --> 00:01:23.288
as I say to myself,
"What is wrong with me?

00:01:24.331 --> 00:01:26.124
I've messed up my kid forever."

00:01:27.083 --> 00:01:30.670
Well, if you're a parent,
you've probably felt that pain.

00:01:31.212 --> 00:01:34.716
For me, it comes
with an extra layer of shame.

00:01:35.508 --> 00:01:37.927
I mean, I’m a clinical psychologist

00:01:37.969 --> 00:01:42.057
and my specialty is helping people
become better parents.

00:01:42.098 --> 00:01:44.559
(Laughter)

00:01:44.601 --> 00:01:46.311
And yet, this is true as well --

00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:50.065
there is no such thing
as a perfect parent.

00:01:50.482 --> 00:01:53.693
Mistakes and struggles,
they come with the job,

00:01:53.693 --> 00:01:56.613
but no one tells us what to do next.

00:01:57.072 --> 00:01:58.657
Do we just move on?

00:01:58.657 --> 00:02:01.618
Kind of just pretend
the whole thing never happened?

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.413
Or if I say something,
what are the words?

00:02:06.081 --> 00:02:10.418
Well, for years, as a clinical
psychologist in private practice,

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.338
I saw client after client
struggle with this question.

00:02:13.713 --> 00:02:18.885
And now, as the creator of the parenting
content and community platform

00:02:18.927 --> 00:02:20.220
"Good Inside,"

00:02:20.261 --> 00:02:25.016
I see millions of parents
around the globe struggle with this issue.

00:02:25.725 --> 00:02:27.936
All parents yell.

00:02:28.645 --> 00:02:31.356
No one knows what to do next.

00:02:32.524 --> 00:02:34.818
Well, I'm determined to fill this gap.

00:02:34.818 --> 00:02:39.280
After all, there's almost nothing
within our interpersonal relationships

00:02:39.322 --> 00:02:42.617
that can have as much impact as repair.

00:02:42.951 --> 00:02:44.744
Whenever a parent asks me,

00:02:44.744 --> 00:02:47.747
"What one parenting strategy
should I focus on?"

00:02:47.789 --> 00:02:50.041
I always say the same thing:

00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:52.085
"Get good at repair."

00:02:52.836 --> 00:02:54.254
So what is repair?

00:02:54.963 --> 00:02:58.800
Repair is the act of going back
to a moment of disconnection.

00:02:59.175 --> 00:03:02.220
Taking responsibility for your behavior

00:03:02.262 --> 00:03:05.640
and acknowledging the impact
it had on another.

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:09.519
And I want to differentiate
a repair from an apology,

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:14.149
because when an apology often looks
to shut a conversation down --

00:03:14.149 --> 00:03:18.069
“Hey, I’m sorry I yelled.
Can we move on now?” --

00:03:18.111 --> 00:03:21.030
a good repair opens one up.

00:03:21.865 --> 00:03:25.744
And if you think about what it means
to get good at repair,

00:03:25.785 --> 00:03:30.874
there's so much baked-in realism
and hope and possibility.

00:03:31.458 --> 00:03:34.919
Repair assumes there's been a rupture.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:37.213
So to repair,

00:03:37.213 --> 00:03:38.923
you have to mess up

00:03:38.923 --> 00:03:42.093
or fall short of someone
else's expectations.

00:03:42.093 --> 00:03:45.305
Which means the next time
I snap at my kid,

00:03:45.346 --> 00:03:48.558
or my husband, or my work colleague,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.185
instead of berating myself,

00:03:50.226 --> 00:03:52.187
like I did that night in the kitchen,

00:03:52.187 --> 00:03:58.401
I try to remind myself I'm focusing
on getting good at repair.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.487
Step one is rupture.

00:04:00.987 --> 00:04:02.697
"Check that off, I crushed it."

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:05.658
(Laughter)

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.202
Step two is repair.

00:04:08.077 --> 00:04:09.245
"I can do this.

00:04:09.662 --> 00:04:11.498
I'm actually right on track."

00:04:13.374 --> 00:04:15.001
So let's get back to my example.

00:04:15.043 --> 00:04:17.170
I'm in the kitchen, my son is in his room.

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:19.923
Well, what will happen if I don't repair?

00:04:20.381 --> 00:04:22.592
That’s really important to understand

00:04:22.592 --> 00:04:25.804
and helps us make a decision
about what to do next.

00:04:27.055 --> 00:04:28.723
Well, here are the facts.

00:04:29.015 --> 00:04:31.518
My son is alone, overwhelmed

00:04:31.518 --> 00:04:33.228
and in a state of distress,

00:04:33.269 --> 00:04:37.899
because, let's face it,
his mom just became scary mom.

00:04:37.941 --> 00:04:40.693
And now, he has to figure out
a way to get back

00:04:40.693 --> 00:04:42.487
to feeling safe and secure.

00:04:43.196 --> 00:04:47.408
And if I don't go help him do that
through making a repair,

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:51.120
he has to rely on one of the only
coping mechanisms

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.456
he has at his own disposal ...

00:04:54.123 --> 00:04:55.667
self-blame.

00:04:56.251 --> 00:04:58.711
Self-blame sounds like this:

00:04:58.711 --> 00:05:00.505
“Something’s wrong with me.

00:05:01.881 --> 00:05:03.174
I’m unlovable.

00:05:04.509 --> 00:05:06.177
I make bad things happen.”

00:05:07.387 --> 00:05:11.683
Ronald Fairbairn may have said it best
when he wrote that, for kids,

00:05:11.724 --> 00:05:16.521
it is better to be a sinner
in a world ruled by God

00:05:16.563 --> 00:05:19.983
than to live in a world
ruled by the devil.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.028
In other words, it's actually adaptive

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:28.449
for a child to internalize
badness and fault,

00:05:28.449 --> 00:05:30.910
because at least then,
they can hold onto the idea

00:05:30.952 --> 00:05:33.454
that their parents
and the world around them

00:05:33.496 --> 00:05:35.540
is safe and good.

00:05:37.292 --> 00:05:40.587
And while self-blame
works for us in childhood,

00:05:40.628 --> 00:05:43.923
we all know it works
against us in adulthood.

00:05:44.591 --> 00:05:46.175
“Something’s wrong with me.

00:05:46.175 --> 00:05:47.677
I make bad things happen.

00:05:47.719 --> 00:05:49.012
I’m unlovable.”

00:05:49.012 --> 00:05:52.473
These are the core fears
of so many adults.

00:05:52.807 --> 00:05:57.270
But really, we see here,
they are actually the childhood stories

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.522
we wrote when we were left alone

00:05:59.564 --> 00:06:03.234
following distressing events
that went unrepaired.

00:06:05.737 --> 00:06:10.116
Plus, adults with self-blame
are vulnerable to depression, anxiety,

00:06:10.158 --> 00:06:11.951
deep feelings of worthlessness --

00:06:11.951 --> 00:06:13.995
none of which we want for our kids.

00:06:14.495 --> 00:06:15.830
And we can do better.

00:06:15.872 --> 00:06:18.416
And it doesn't mean we have to be perfect.

00:06:18.458 --> 00:06:19.751
When you repair,

00:06:19.751 --> 00:06:23.796
you go further than removing
a child's story of self-blame.

00:06:24.172 --> 00:06:29.677
You get to add in all the elements
that were missing in the first place.

00:06:29.719 --> 00:06:35.308
Safety, connection, coherence,
love, goodness.

00:06:35.642 --> 00:06:37.518
It's as if you're saying to a child,

00:06:37.518 --> 00:06:41.814
"I will not let this chapter
of your life end in self-blame.

00:06:42.815 --> 00:06:46.819
Yes, this chapter will still contain
the event of yelling,

00:06:46.819 --> 00:06:50.907
but I can ensure this chapter
has a different ending,

00:06:50.949 --> 00:06:55.995
and therefore a different title,
and theme and lesson learned."

00:06:56.955 --> 00:07:00.208
We know that memory is original events

00:07:00.208 --> 00:07:04.003
combined with every other time
you've remembered that event.

00:07:04.837 --> 00:07:07.131
This is why therapy's helpful, right?

00:07:07.173 --> 00:07:10.927
When you remember painful
experiences from your past

00:07:10.927 --> 00:07:15.056
within a safer and more
connected relationship,

00:07:15.098 --> 00:07:16.557
the event remains,

00:07:16.599 --> 00:07:22.271
but your story of the event,
it changes, and then you change.

00:07:23.272 --> 00:07:24.649
With repair,

00:07:24.691 --> 00:07:27.568
we effectively change the past.

00:07:29.362 --> 00:07:31.406
So let's write a better story.

00:07:31.406 --> 00:07:33.157
Let's learn how to repair.

00:07:33.992 --> 00:07:36.703
Step one, repair with yourself.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:38.287
That's right.

00:07:38.287 --> 00:07:40.373
I mean, you can't offer compassion

00:07:40.415 --> 00:07:44.168
or groundedness or understanding
to someone else

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:47.964
before you access
those qualities within yourself.

00:07:48.715 --> 00:07:52.051
Self-repair means
separating your identity,

00:07:52.093 --> 00:07:53.886
who you are,

00:07:53.886 --> 00:07:57.432
from your behavior, what you did.

00:07:58.349 --> 00:08:02.020
For me, it means telling myself
two things are true.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:04.772
I’m not proud of my latest behavior

00:08:04.814 --> 00:08:07.525
and my latest behavior doesn't define me.

00:08:08.026 --> 00:08:13.114
Even as I struggle on the outside,
I remain good inside.

00:08:14.115 --> 00:08:16.868
I can then start to see
that I'm a good parent --

00:08:16.868 --> 00:08:18.036
identity --

00:08:18.036 --> 00:08:21.789
who was having a hard time -- behavior.

00:08:21.831 --> 00:08:25.752
And no, this doesn't let me off the hook.

00:08:25.793 --> 00:08:29.505
This is precisely what leaves me
on the hook for change.

00:08:29.547 --> 00:08:33.009
Because now that I've replaced
my spiral with groundedness,

00:08:33.051 --> 00:08:34.761
I can actually use my energy

00:08:34.761 --> 00:08:38.306
toward thinking about what I want
to do differently the next time.

00:08:38.347 --> 00:08:41.434
Oh, and I can now use my energy
to go repair with my son.

00:08:41.976 --> 00:08:44.312
Step two -- repair with your child.

00:08:44.771 --> 00:08:46.230
There's no exact formula.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:48.357
I often think about three elements:

00:08:48.399 --> 00:08:51.069
name what happened, take responsibility,

00:08:51.069 --> 00:08:53.488
state what you would do
differently the next time.

00:08:53.488 --> 00:08:55.531
It could come together like this.

00:08:55.573 --> 00:08:56.908
"Hey.

00:08:56.949 --> 00:09:00.161
I keep thinking about what happened
the other night in the kitchen.

00:09:00.161 --> 00:09:01.704
I'm sorry I yelled.

00:09:02.121 --> 00:09:04.040
I'm sure that felt scary.

00:09:04.082 --> 00:09:05.750
And it wasn't your fault.

00:09:06.125 --> 00:09:09.003
I'm working on staying calm,
even when I'm frustrated."

00:09:10.004 --> 00:09:14.300
A 15-second intervention
can have a lifelong impact.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.804
I've replaced my child's
story of self-blame

00:09:17.845 --> 00:09:21.766
with a story of self-trust
and safety and connection.

00:09:21.766 --> 00:09:23.851
I mean, what a massive upgrade.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.647
And to give a little more clarity
around how to repair,

00:09:27.688 --> 00:09:31.651
I want to share a few examples
of what I call "not repair,"

00:09:31.692 --> 00:09:34.487
which are things that come
more naturally to most of us --

00:09:34.529 --> 00:09:36.197
definitely me included.

00:09:36.572 --> 00:09:39.033
"Hey, I'm sorry I yelled at you
in the kitchen,

00:09:39.075 --> 00:09:41.410
but if you wouldn't have
complained about dinner,

00:09:41.452 --> 00:09:42.745
it wouldn't have happened."

00:09:42.745 --> 00:09:43.788
(Laughter)

00:09:43.788 --> 00:09:45.498
Been there? Been there? OK.

00:09:45.498 --> 00:09:49.127
Or "You know, you really need to be
grateful for things in your life,

00:09:49.168 --> 00:09:50.545
like a home-cooked meal.

00:09:50.545 --> 00:09:52.130
Then, you won't get yelled at."

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:53.673
(Laughter)

00:09:54.298 --> 00:09:57.969
Not only do these interventions
fail at the goal of reconnection,

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:02.014
they also insinuate that your child
caused your reaction,

00:10:02.056 --> 00:10:03.808
which simply isn’t true

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:09.063
and isn't a model of emotion regulation
we want to pass on to the next generation.

00:10:09.105 --> 00:10:12.608
(Applause)

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:14.360
So let's say we've all resisted

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:17.280
the "it was your fault,
anyway" not-repairs,

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:21.909
and have instead prioritized
a repair that allows us to reconnect.

00:10:21.909 --> 00:10:26.539
What might the impact be?
What might that look like in adulthood?

00:10:27.331 --> 00:10:31.627
My adult child won’t spiral in self-blame
when they make a mistake,

00:10:31.627 --> 00:10:34.839
and won’t take on blame
for someone else’s mistake.

00:10:35.631 --> 00:10:39.844
My adult child will know how to take
responsibility for their behavior,

00:10:39.844 --> 00:10:44.140
because you've modeled
how to take responsibility for yours.

00:10:45.433 --> 00:10:46.976
Repairing with a child today

00:10:46.976 --> 00:10:50.897
sets the stage for these critical
adult relationship patterns.

00:10:51.647 --> 00:10:53.191
Plus, it gets better --

00:10:53.232 --> 00:10:59.113
now that I've reconnected with my son,
I can do something really impactful.

00:10:59.113 --> 00:11:02.450
I can teach him a skill
he didn't have in the first place,

00:11:02.491 --> 00:11:05.578
which is how kids
actually change their behavior.

00:11:06.245 --> 00:11:07.413
So maybe the next day,

00:11:07.455 --> 00:11:10.958
I say "You know, you're not always
going to like what I make for dinner.

00:11:10.958 --> 00:11:13.211
Instead of saying 'that's disgusting,'

00:11:13.211 --> 00:11:16.130
I wonder if you could say
'not my favorite.'"

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:20.927
Now I'm teaching him how to regulate
his understandable disappointment,

00:11:20.968 --> 00:11:24.680
and communicate effectively
and respectfully with another person.

00:11:24.722 --> 00:11:27.391
That never would have happened if instead,

00:11:27.433 --> 00:11:30.019
I had been blaming him for my reaction.

00:11:31.187 --> 00:11:34.148
So here's the point where you might have
a lingering concern.

00:11:34.148 --> 00:11:35.524
Maybe you're thinking,

00:11:35.524 --> 00:11:38.694
"You know, I have a feeling
that my kid's older than your kid."

00:11:38.694 --> 00:11:40.112
(Laughter)

00:11:40.154 --> 00:11:41.822
"I think it's too late."

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:46.369
Or "I have done a lot worse
than you did in the kitchen."

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:47.411
(Laughter)

00:11:47.453 --> 00:11:48.913
"Maybe it's too late."

00:11:49.413 --> 00:11:50.581
Well, I mean this --

00:11:50.581 --> 00:11:52.708
if you have only one takeaway
from this talk,

00:11:52.708 --> 00:11:55.044
please let this be it:
It is not too late.

00:11:55.336 --> 00:11:57.505
It is never too late.

00:11:58.297 --> 00:11:59.423
How do I know?

00:12:00.633 --> 00:12:05.054
Well, imagine, right after this,
you get a call from one of your parents,

00:12:05.054 --> 00:12:07.098
and if neither of your parents are alive,

00:12:07.098 --> 00:12:10.768
imagine finding and opening a letter
you hadn't seen till that moment.

00:12:11.602 --> 00:12:13.896
OK, walk through this
with me, here's the call.

00:12:14.981 --> 00:12:16.357
"Hey,

00:12:16.399 --> 00:12:18.401
I know this sounds out of the blue,

00:12:19.193 --> 00:12:21.988
but I've been thinking a lot
about your childhood.

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:28.035
And I think there were a lot of moments
that felt really bad to you.

00:12:29.412 --> 00:12:31.747
And you are right to feel that way.

00:12:33.582 --> 00:12:36.043
Those moments weren't your fault.

00:12:36.043 --> 00:12:37.962
They were times when I was struggling,

00:12:38.004 --> 00:12:40.798
and if I could have gone back,
I would have stepped aside,

00:12:40.798 --> 00:12:42.383
I would have calmed myself down,

00:12:42.383 --> 00:12:46.095
and then found you to help you
with whatever you were struggling with.

00:12:46.512 --> 00:12:47.638
I'm sorry.

00:12:48.764 --> 00:12:52.018
And if you're ever willing to talk to me
about any of those moments,

00:12:52.059 --> 00:12:53.227
I'll listen.

00:12:53.269 --> 00:12:57.106
I won't listen to have a rebuttal.
I'll listen to understand.

00:12:57.773 --> 00:12:58.899
I love you."

00:12:59.650 --> 00:13:02.820
I don't know many adults
who don't have a fairly visceral reaction

00:13:02.862 --> 00:13:04.322
to that exercise.

00:13:04.322 --> 00:13:07.450
I often hear, “Why am I crying?”

00:13:08.159 --> 00:13:11.162
Or "Listen, that wouldn't
change everything.

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:14.707
But it might change some things."

00:13:15.499 --> 00:13:19.003
Well, I definitely
do not specialize in math,

00:13:19.045 --> 00:13:21.130
but here's something
I know with certainty.

00:13:21.756 --> 00:13:26.302
If you have a child,
that child is younger than you are.

00:13:26.761 --> 00:13:28.888
(Laughter)

00:13:28.888 --> 00:13:30.056
Always true.

00:13:31.891 --> 00:13:34.143
The story of their life is shorter

00:13:34.185 --> 00:13:36.979
and even more amenable to editing.

00:13:38.272 --> 00:13:42.943
So if that imagined exercise
had an impact on you,

00:13:42.943 --> 00:13:48.115
imagine the impact an actual repair
will have on your child.

00:13:49.241 --> 00:13:50.409
See?

00:13:50.409 --> 00:13:52.787
I told you, it's never too late.

00:13:53.788 --> 00:13:54.914
Thank you.

00:13:54.955 --> 00:14:01.962
(Cheers and applause)

